From 0b9d9d01d5a58051096da5272448b2a7aa582b75 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 21:29:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(model): source the regulated rates, band them by plant
 size, and flag the regime that ends them
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Research against the publishing bodies rather than the repo. Three of the
calculator's regulated inputs were wrong, and the assumption underneath all of
them has an expiry date nobody had noticed.

WRONG NUMBERS
- Einspeisevergütung was a flat 7,86 ct/kWh. The published Teileinspeisung rate
  for a 30 kWp plant in the current window is 6,66. The model overstated feed-in
  revenue for every project in the 10-40 kWp band, which is most of them.
- Mieterstromzuschlag was a flat 2,50 ct/kWh, when the rate is banded and falls
  to 1,29 above 40 kWp.
- CO2 factor was 0,38 t/MWh against the Umweltbundesamt figure of 0,344 for
  2025 — a 10 % overstatement of the headline environmental number.

Worse than any single figure: all three are BANDED BY PLANT SIZE and the engine
took scalars, so changing kWp silently kept a rate belonging to a different
plant. shared/tariffs.ts now holds the bands, refuses to extrapolate above the
published schedule, and carries validity windows — a rate quoted without its
window goes stale silently.

Effect on the shipped default scenario, re-derived from an INDEPENDENT Python
reimplementation of the model (not a copy of the code under test):
    NPV  22.151,81 -> 19.463,26 EUR   (-12,1 %)
    IRR       9,56 -> 8,94 %
    CO2      92,98 -> 84,17 t         (-9,5 %)
The site had been overstating its own default case by roughly a tenth. The
engine math is unchanged; only its inputs were wrong. ASSUMPTION_SET bumped to
de-mieterstrom-2026-09.2 so old and new reports are not silently comparable.

THE ASSUMPTION WITH AN EXPIRY DATE
The 20-year model rests on a fixed feed-in tariff running the whole term. The
EEG 2027 government draft abolishes it for new plants: under 25 kW lose EEG
support entirely and face a permanent 50 % feed-in cap; 25-100 kW get
market-value pass-through with no subsidy element; 100 kW and above move to a
two-sided CfD with an annual claw-back. For a plant commissioned under that
regime this model is not miscalibrated, it is the wrong SHAPE.

regimeWarning() states this, and the eligibility engine raises it for any plant
that is only planned. Deliberately severity "info", not "review": it applies to
every planned plant, so escalating the verdict on it would make REQUIRES_REVIEW
meaningless. It surfaces as the main risk instead. It also says plainly that
self-consumption is untouched and gains in relative importance — the wedge
points at the right thing.

SOURCING DISCIPLINE
A value is verified: true only when read from the body that publishes it. The
UBA emission factor qualifies. The BNetzA and EEG rates were read from secondary
summaries, so they carry reference and date but stay unverified — "someone
reported the regulator's number" is not "the regulator published this number".

The § 42a price cap (90 % of the local basic-supply tariff) is now modelled as a
check that answers "unknown" without the local tariff rather than inventing a
reference price — same discipline the eligibility engine uses for missing facts.

The citation guard from 0003 caught this patch's own new code, which is what it
is for. Rather than widening the allow-list, the duplicated paragraph was
removed: the price cap now references the model register instead of restating
it, and the guard pins the register list at exactly two so a third cannot be
added quietly to dodge it.

Evidence: pnpm check clean, 194/194 vitest (17 new tariff + 5 new regime + 3 new
sourcing assertions), pnpm build clean.

